// ============================================================================
// lib/option.bx — absence, made explicit.
//
//     use "lib/option.bx";
//
// Burxt has no null. Nothing is implicitly absent, no value silently means
// "missing", and no dereference can fail — because absence is a type, and the
// compiler makes you say what happens when there is nothing there.
//
// This is a LIBRARY file. `Option<T>` is four lines of Burxt with no compiler
// support beyond generics, which is the test M7 set for whether the generics are
// real: if `Option` had needed a keyword, they were not.
// ============================================================================

// One of two things: nothing, or a value. `match` forces both cases to be
// written, so "I forgot to handle missing" is a compile error rather than a
// crash at three in the morning.
enum Option<T> {
    None,
    Some(T)
}

// ---- Reading one -----------------------------------------------------------
//
// `match` is the whole interface, and it is deliberately not hidden:
//
//     match found {
//         None => { print("nothing"); }
//         Some(value) => { print(value); }
//     }
//
// What the helpers below add is the two shapes worth naming. They take the
// fallback by value, so nothing is computed that might not be needed — Burxt has
// no closures yet, and a lazy default would need one.

// The value, or the fallback. The one everybody writes first.
function option_or<T>(o: Option<T>, fallback: T) -> T {
    match o {
        None => { return fallback; }
        Some(value) => { return value; }
    }
}

// Whether there is anything there. Useful in a condition; useless for getting at
// the value, which is the point — asking is not the same as having.
function option_is_some<T>(o: Option<T>) -> Bool {
    match o {
        None => { return false; }
        Some(value) => { return true; }
    }
}

function option_is_none<T>(o: Option<T>) -> Bool {
    match o {
        None => { return true; }
        Some(value) => { return false; }
    }
}

// ---- What is deliberately absent -------------------------------------------
//
// There is no `unwrap`. A function that says "give me the value and abort if
// there is none" is null with extra steps, and the crash it causes is exactly
// the one this type exists to prevent. `option_or` covers the case where a
// default is right, and `match` covers the case where it is not.
//
// There is no `map`. It needs a function as a value, and a closure needs an
// owner for its captured state — see spec/M10-ERGONOMICS.md §3.
//
// `?` does work here: in a function that itself answers with an `Option`,
// `let at: Int = index_of(xs, 8)?;` yields the value or returns `None` immediately. It
// recognises absence by the variant name `None`, so nothing in the compiler needs
// to know this file exists.
//
// A payload may be a scalar, a String, a record or an array. `Option<Point>` works since
// v0.0.118 — the layout was one tag cell plus the widest variant all along. Another ENUM is
// still refused, because an enum inside an enum has no finite size without indirection.
